# rp2350_touch_lcd_28

A Waveshare RP2350-Touch-LCD-2.8: an RP2350A with 16MB of flash carrying a 240
by 320 IPS panel on an ST7789, a five-point CST328 capacitive touch controller,
a QMI8658 inertial sensor, a PCF85063 clock, and a PCM5101A DAC driving an
APA2068 amplifier and a speaker.

It derives from pico2-arm because that is what it is: the same RP2350A the Pico
SDK configures as pico2. The SDK publishes no board header for this product, and
Waveshare's own firmware for it selects PICO_BOARD pico2 and drives every
peripheral by explicit GPIO number, which is exactly what deriving does here.
The chip, the SDK, and the ABI come from the base; the six bindings above and
the pin maps in the providers are what this board adds.

The panel is wired to SPI1 -- clock GP10, data GP11, CS GP13, DC GP14, reset
GP15, backlight GP16 -- and the touch controller, the IMU, and the clock share
I2C1 on GP6 and GP7, the touch controller's reset and interrupt on GP17 and
GP18. The DAC's I2S link is bit clock GP2, word clock GP3, and data GP4; its
audio provider, platform code rather than board description, lives under
platforms/pico/rp2350_touch_lcd_28/audio. GP25 is the battery key, GP26 the
battery enable, GP27 the battery sense, and GP19 to GP24 the TF card -- clock,
CMD, and DAT0 to DAT3 -- which platform.rp2350_touch_lcd_28.sdcard publishes
as mm.sdcard.socket in 4-bit SD mode over PIO, since hardware SPI cannot reach
those pins; GP0 and GP1 are the exposed UART.

The Pico bridge's board table corrects two pico2 defaults for this board:
GP25 is a key, not an LED, so the board reports no LED, and ADC_AVDD is the 3V3
rail, so the ADC reference is 3300 mV.

Every pin above was checked against Waveshare's schematic,
RP2350-Touch-LCD-2.8-Schematic.pdf, read directly from files.waveshare.com;
the providers' earlier pin maps, taken from a third-party board package,
agree with it. None of it is qualified on hardware.

https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/RP2350-Touch-LCD-2.8
